 ],
 "answer_text": "Found 3 gene records for Li-Fraumeni syndrome: tumor protein p53 [1], cyclin dependent kinase inhibitor 2A [2] and checkpoint kinase 2 [3], linked to 1 disease: Li-Fraumeni syndrome [6]. \n\nLi-Fraumeni syndrome is associated with the tumor protein p53 gene (NCBIGene:7157) [1], the cyclin dependent kinase inhibitor 2A gene (NCBIGene:1029) [2], and the checkpoint kinase 2 gene (NCBIGene:11200) [3]. \n\nThe syndrome follows an autosomal dominant inheritance pattern with high penetrance and confers a very high lifetime cumulative cancer risk [4]. \n\nAdditional Associated Genes\n\nClinical heterogeneity observed across families carrying the same TP53 pathogenic variant may be explained by polymorphic variants in TP53-related genes, among other factors [5]. \n\nDisease records found\n\nDisease name: Li-Fraumeni syndrome [6]. \n\nGene-to-disease mapping\n\nGene NCBIGene:1029, name: cyclin dependent kinase inhibitor 2A [2]. Gene NCBIGene:11200, name: checkpoint kinase 2 [3]. Gene NCBIGene:7157, name: tumor protein p53 [1]. \n\nPubmed records found\n\nPubmed title: Li-Fraumeni Syndrome [7]. Pubmed title: Li-Fraumeni syndrome [12]. Pubmed title: Li-Fraumeni Syndrome [14]. Pubmed title: Li-Fraumeni syndrome heterogeneity [16]. \n\nMedgen records found\n\nMedgen title: Li-Fraumeni syndrome [8]. Medgen clinical_features: MedGen lists no clinical features for Li-Fraumeni syndrome [9]. \n\nLiterature entity records found\n\nLiterature entity name: Li-Fraumeni Syndrome [10]. \n\nClinical trial records found\n\nClinical trial name: Clinical, Genetic, Behavioral, Laboratory and Epidemiologic Characterization of Individuals and Families at High Risk of Breast/Ovarian Cancer [11]. Clinical trial name: Role of p53 Gene in Metabolism Regulation in Patients With Li-Fraumeni Syndrome [13]. Clinical trial name: Characterization of Patients With Uncommon Presentations and/or Uncommon Diseases Associated With the Cardiovascular System [15]. Clinical trial name: Clinical and Genetic Studies of Li-Fraumeni Syndrome [17]. Clinical trial name: LIFSCREEN : Evaluation of Whole Body MRI for Early Detection of Cancers in Subjects With P53 Mutation (Li-Fraumeni Syndrome) [18]. "
